Currently I use ASCII graphics, but I switched just because
I wanted to play on Linux and can't stand the QT version of nethack.
Before I used tiles and played the dos version on FreeDos.

About portability:  it's not hard to make your code portable as long as
you pay attention to it from the beginning.  So just keep the code 
portable as you are writing your game.  There is nothing worse than
deciding to port your game to another platform when it's 10000+ lines
long.
As you are making a roguelike, I'd say you won't be writing a lot of
system-specific code.  So just put things like reading directories, 
input and output in separated funcions, and you won't have much trouble
later.  I'd also suggest using tools and libraries that are available on
other systems (DJGPP, maybe Allegro).  Unfortunately ncurses aren't 
available on windows (or am I wrong?).  If you want to use the ms tools
like vbasic or builder, you might have much trouble with porting.

good luck with your game

